        <definition>std::vector&lt; std::unique_ptr&lt; BufDataType &gt; &gt; AGRemapCore::VbFile::parseFormatName</definition>
        <argsstring>(const std::string &amp;formatName)</argsstring>
        <name>parseFormatName</name>
        <qualifiedname>AGRemapCore::VbFile::parseFormatName</qualifiedname>
        <param>
          <type>const std::string &amp;</type>
          <declname>formatName</declname>
        </param>
        <briefdescription>
        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para><verbatim>embed:rst 
Builds the data types making up an element from the `DXGI format`_ name a dump&apos;s header
gives for it :raw-html:`&lt;br /&gt;` :raw-html:`&lt;br /&gt;`

The channels decide *how many* data types there are and how wide each one is, and the
suffix decides what kind they are -- so ``R32G32B32_FLOAT`` is 3 four-byte floats,
``R8G8B8A8_UNORM`` is 4 one-byte `unsigned normalized integers`_, and
``R32G32B32A32_SINT`` is 4 four-byte signed integers
</verbatim></para>
<para><parameterlist kind="param"><parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>formatName</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>The format name to parse, eg. <computeroutput>"R32G32B32_FLOAT"</computeroutput></para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
</parameterlist>
<simplesect kind="return"><para>The data types making up the element. Empty if the format is not one this understands </para>
</simplesect>
</para>
        </detaileddescription>
        <inbodydescription>
        </inbodydescription>
        <location file="include/AGRemapCore/model/files/VbFile.h" line="131" column="32" bodyfile="src/model/files/VbFile.cpp" bodystart="120" bodyend="183"/>
      </memberdef>
      <memberdef kind="function" id="class_a_g_remap_core_1_1_vb_file_1ae6f10c050865926e4dbd124a1329db8f" prot="public" static="yes" const="no" explicit="no" inline="no" virt="non-virtual">
        <type>std::vector&lt; std::unique_ptr&lt; <ref refid="class_a_g_remap_core_1_1_buf_element_type" kindref="compound">BufElementType</ref> &gt; &gt;</type>
        <definition>std::vector&lt; std::unique_ptr&lt; BufElementType &gt; &gt; AGRemapCore::VbFile::parseDumpHeader</definition>
        <argsstring>(const std::string &amp;text)</argsstring>
        <name>parseDumpHeader</name>
        <qualifiedname>AGRemapCore::VbFile::parseDumpHeader</qualifiedname>
        <param>
          <type>const std::string &amp;</type>
          <declname>text</declname>
        </param>
        <briefdescription>
        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para><verbatim>embed:rst 
Builds the elements of a vertex line out of a dumped *vb.txt* file&apos;s header
:raw-html:`&lt;br /&gt;` :raw-html:`&lt;br /&gt;`

The header names each element and gives its `DXGI format`_, which together are
everything a ``.vb`` file needs -- so a dump can be read back without being told what
its layout was
</verbatim></para>
<para><parameterlist kind="param"><parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>text</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>The text of the dumped <emphasis>vb.txt</emphasis> file</para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
</parameterlist>
<simplesect kind="return"><para>The elements the header declares, in byte order. Empty when the text has no header to read them from, or when one of its formats could not be parsed </para>
</simplesect>
</para>
        </detaileddescription>
        <inbodydescription>
        </inbodydescription>
        <location file="include/AGRemapCore/model/files/VbFile.h" line="149" column="32" bodyfile="src/model/files/VbFile.cpp" bodystart="185" bodyend="211"/>
      </memberdef>
    </sectiondef>
    <briefdescription>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para><verbatim>embed:rst 
This class inherits from :cpp:class:`BufFile` :raw-html:`&lt;br /&gt;` :raw-html:`&lt;br /&gt;`

Used for handling ``.vb`` (vertex buffer) files :raw-html:`&lt;br /&gt;` :raw-html:`&lt;br /&gt;`

.. note::
   A GI character&apos;s ``.vb`` data does not live in one file -- it is split across a
   ``Position.buf``, a ``Blend.buf`` and a ``Texcoord.buf``, one line each per vertex. Use
   :cpp:func:`BufFile::merge` to stitch such a set back together, which fills in both the bytes
   and the elements
</verbatim> </para>
    </detaileddescription>
